[31m-# gsap.utils[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## When to Use This Skill[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Apply when writing or reviewing code that uses **gsap.utils** for math, array/collection handling, unit parsing, or value mapping in animations (e.g. mapping scroll to a value, randomizing, snapping to a grid, or normalizing inputs).[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Related skills:** Use with **gsap-core**, **gsap-timeline**, and **gsap-scrolltrigger** when building animations; CustomEase and other easing utilities are in **gsap-plugins**.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Overview[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**gsap.utils** provides pure helpers; no need to register. Use in tween vars (e.g. function-based values), in ScrollTrigger or Observer callbacks, or in any JS that drives GSAP. All are on **gsap.utils** (e.g. `gsap.utils.clamp()`).[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Omitting the value: function form.** Many utils accept the value to transform as the **last** argument. If you omit that argument, the util returns a **function** that accepts the value later. Use the function form when you need to clamp, map, normalize, or snap many values with the same config (e.g. in a mousemove handler or tween callback). **Exception: random()** — pass **true** as the last argument to get a reusable function (do not omit the value); see [random()](https://gsap.com/docs/v3/GSAP/UtilityMethods/random()).[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-// With value: returns the result[m
[31m-gsap.utils.clamp(0, 100, 150); // 100[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// Without value: returns a function you call with the value later[m
[31m-let c = gsap.utils.clamp(0, 100);[m
[31m-c(150);  // 100[m
[31m-c(-10);  // 0[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Clamping and Ranges[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### clamp(min, max, value?)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Constrains a value between min and max. Omit **value** to get a function: `clamp(min, max)(value)`.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.utils.clamp(0, 100, 150); // 100[m
[31m-gsap.utils.clamp(0, 100, -10); // 0[m
[31m-[m
[31m-let clampFn = gsap.utils.clamp(0, 100);[m
[31m-clampFn(150); // 100[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### mapRange(inMin, inMax, outMin, outMax, value?)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Maps a value from one range to another. Use when converting scroll position, progress (0–1), or input range to an animation range. Omit **value** to get a function: `mapRange(inMin, inMax, outMin, outMax)(value)`.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.utils.mapRange(0, 100, 0, 500, 50);  // 250[m
[31m-gsap.utils.mapRange(0, 1, 0, 360, 0.5);   // 180 (progress to degrees)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-let mapFn = gsap.utils.mapRange(0, 100, 0, 500);[m
[31m-mapFn(50);  // 250[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### normalize(min, max, value?)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Returns a value normalized to 0–1 for the given range. Inverse of mapping when the target range is 0–1. Omit **value** to get a function: `normalize(min, max)(value)`.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.utils.normalize(0, 100, 50);   // 0.5[m
[31m-gsap.utils.normalize(100, 300, 200); // 0.5[m
[31m-[m
[31m-let normFn = gsap.utils.normalize(0, 100);[m
[31m-normFn(50); // 0.5[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### interpolate(start, end, progress?)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Interpolates between two values at a given progress (0–1). Handles numbers, colors, and objects with matching keys. Omit **progress** to get a function: `interpolate(start, end)(progress)`.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.utils.interpolate(0, 100, 0.5);       // 50[m
[31m-gsap.utils.interpolate("#ff0000", "#0000ff", 0.5); // mid color[m
[31m-gsap.utils.interpolate({ x: 0, y: 0 }, { x: 100, y: 50 }, 0.5); // { x: 50, y: 25 }[m
[31m-[m
[31m-let lerp = gsap.utils.interpolate(0, 100);[m
[31m-lerp(0.5); // 50[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Random and Snap[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### random(minimum, maximum[, snapIncrement, returnFunction]) / random(array[, returnFunction])[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Returns a random number in the range **minimum**–**maximum**, or a random element from an **array**. Optional **snapIncrement** snaps the result to the nearest multiple (e.g. `5` → multiples of 5). **To get a reusable function**, pass **true** as the last argument (**returnFunction**); the returned function takes no args and returns a new random value each time. This is the only util that uses `true` for the function form instead of omitting the value.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-// immediate value: number in range[m
[31m-gsap.utils.random(-100, 100);        // e.g. 42.7[m
[31m-gsap.utils.random(0, 500, 5);        // 0–500, snapped to nearest 5[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// reusable function: pass true as last argument[m
[31m-let randomFn = gsap.utils.random(-200, 500, 10, true);[m
[31m-randomFn();  // random value in range, snapped to 10[m
[31m-randomFn();  // another random value[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// array: pick one value at random[m
[31m-gsap.utils.random(["red", "blue", "green"]);  // "red", "blue", or "green"[m
[31m-let randomFromArray = gsap.utils.random([0, 100, 200], true);[m
[31m-randomFromArray();  // 0, 100, or 200[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**String form in tween vars:** use `"random(-100, 100)"`, `"random(-100, 100, 5)"`, or `"random([0, 100, 200])"`; GSAP evaluates it per target.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.to(".box", { x: "random(-100, 100, 5)", duration: 1 });[m
[31m-gsap.to(".item", { backgroundColor: "random([red, blue, green])" });[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### snap(snapTo, value?)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Snaps a value to the nearest multiple of **snapTo**, or to the nearest value in an array of allowed values. Omit **value** to get a function: `snap(snapTo)(value)` (or `snap(snapArray)(value)`).[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.utils.snap(10, 23);     // 20[m
[31m-gsap.utils.snap(0.25, 0.7);  // 0.75[m
[31m-gsap.utils.snap([0, 100, 200], 150); // 100 or 200 (nearest in array)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-let snapFn = gsap.utils.snap(10);[m
[31m-snapFn(23); // 20[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Use in tweens for grid or step-based animation:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.to(".x", { x: 200, snap: { x: 20 } });[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### shuffle(array)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Returns a new array with the same elements in random order. Use for randomizing order (e.g. stagger from "random" with a copy).[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.utils.shuffle([1, 2, 3, 4]); // e.g. [3, 1, 4, 2][m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### distribute(config)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Returns a function** that assigns a value to each target based on its position in the array (or in a grid). Used internally for advanced staggers; use it whenever you need values spread across many elements (e.g. scale, opacity, x, delay). The returned function receives `(index, target, targets)` — either call it manually or pass the result directly into a tween; GSAP will call it per target with index, element, and array.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Config (all optional):**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-| Property | Type | Description |[m
[31m-|----------|------|-------------|[m
[31m-| `base` | Number | Starting value. Default `0`. |[m
[31m-| `amount` | Number | Total to distribute across all targets (added to base). E.g. `amount: 1` with 100 targets → 0.01 between each. Use **each** instead to set a fixed step per target. |[m
[31m-| `each` | Number | Amount to add between each target (added to base). E.g. `each: 1` with 4 targets → 0, 1, 2, 3. Use **amount** instead to split a total. |[m
[31m-| `from` | Number \| String \| Array | Where distribution starts: index, or `"start"`, `"center"`, `"edges"`, `"random"`, `"end"`, or ratios like `[0.25, 0.75]`. Default `0`. |[m
[31m-| `grid` | String \| Array | Use grid position instead of flat index: `[rows, columns]` (e.g. `[5, 10]`) or `"auto"` to detect. Omit for flat array. |[m
[31m-| `axis` | String | For grid: limit to one axis (`"x"` or `"y"`). |[m
[31m-| `ease` | Ease | Distribute values along an ease curve (e.g. `"power1.inOut"`). Default `"none"`. |[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**In a tween:** pass the result of `distribute(config)` as the property value; GSAP calls the function for each target with `(index, target, targets)`.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-// Scale: middle elements 0.5, outer edges 3 (amount 2.5 distributed from center)[m
[31m-gsap.to(".class", {[m
[31m-  scale: gsap.utils.distribute({[m
[31m-    base: 0.5,[m
[31m-    amount: 2.5,[m
[31m-    from: "center"[m
[31m-  })[m
[31m-});[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Manual use:** call the returned function with `(index, target, targets)` to get the value for that index.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-const distributor = gsap.utils.distribute({[m
[31m-  base: 50,[m
[31m-  amount: 100,[m
[31m-  from: "center",[m
[31m-  ease: "power1.inOut"[m
[31m-});[m
[31m-const targets = gsap.utils.toArray(".box");[m
[31m-const valueForIndex2 = distributor(2, targets[2], targets);[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-See [distribute()](https://gsap.com/docs/v3/GSAP/UtilityMethods/distribute/) for more.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Units and Parsing[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### getUnit(value)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Returns the unit string of a value (e.g. `"px"`, `"%"`, `"deg"`). Use when normalizing or converting values.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.utils.getUnit("100px");   // "px"[m
[31m-gsap.utils.getUnit("50%");     // "%"[m
[31m-gsap.utils.getUnit(42);        // "" (unitless)[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### unitize(value, unit)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Appends a unit to a number, or returns the value as-is if it already has a unit. Use when building CSS values or tween end values.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.utils.unitize(100, "px");  // "100px"[m
[31m-gsap.utils.unitize("2rem", "px"); // "2rem" (unchanged)[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### splitColor(color, returnHSL?)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Converts a color string into an array: **[red, green, blue]** (0–255), or **[red, green, blue, alpha]** (4 elements for RGBA when alpha is present or required). Pass **true** as the second argument (**returnHSL**) to get **[hue, saturation, lightness]** or **[hue, saturation, lightness, alpha]** (HSL/HSLA) instead. Works with `"rgb()"`, `"rgba()"`, `"hsl()"`, `"hsla()"`, hex, and named colors (e.g. `"red"`). Use when animating color components or building gradients. See [splitColor()](https://gsap.com/docs/v3/GSAP/UtilityMethods/splitColor/).[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.utils.splitColor("red");                    // [255, 0, 0][m
[31m-gsap.utils.splitColor("#6fb936");                // [111, 185, 54][m
[31m-gsap.utils.splitColor("rgba(204, 153, 51, 0.5)"); // [204, 153, 51, 0.5] (4 elements)[m
[31m-gsap.utils.splitColor("#6fb936", true);          // [94, 55, 47] (HSL: hue, saturation, lightness)[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Arrays and Collections[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### selector(scope)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Returns a scoped selector function that finds elements only within the given element (or ref). Use in components so selectors like `".box"` match only descendants of that component, not the whole document. Accepts a DOM element or a ref (e.g. React ref; handles `.current`).[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-const q = gsap.utils.selector(containerRef);[m
[31m-q(".box");        // array of .box elements inside container[m
[31m-gsap.to(q(".circle"), { x: 100 });[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### toArray(value, scope?)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Converts a value to an array: selector string (scoped to element), NodeList, HTMLCollection, single element, or array. Use when passing mixed inputs to GSAP (e.g. targets) and a true array is needed.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.utils.toArray(".item");           // array of elements[m
[31m-gsap.utils.toArray(".item", container); // scoped to container[m
[31m-gsap.utils.toArray(nodeList);          // [ ... ] from NodeList[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### pipe(...functions)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Composes functions: **pipe(f1, f2, f3)(value)** returns f3(f2(f1(value))). Use when applying a chain of transforms (e.g. normalize → mapRange → snap) in a tween or callback.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-const fn = gsap.utils.pipe([m
[31m-  (v) => gsap.utils.normalize(0, 100, v),[m
[31m-  (v) => gsap.utils.snap(0.1, v)[m
[31m-);[m
[31m-fn(50); // normalized then snapped[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### wrap(min, max, value?)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Wraps a value into the range min–max (inclusive min, exclusive max). Use for infinite scroll or cyclic values. Omit **value** to get a function: `wrap(min, max)(value)`.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.utils.wrap(0, 360, 370);  // 10[m
[31m-gsap.utils.wrap(0, 360, -10);   // 350[m
[31m-[m
[31m-let wrapFn = gsap.utils.wrap(0, 360);[m
[31m-wrapFn(370); // 10[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### wrapYoyo(min, max, value?)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Wraps value in range with a yoyo (bounces at ends). Use for back-and-forth within a range. Omit **value** to get a function: `wrapYoyo(min, max)(value)`.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-gsap.utils.wrapYoyo(0, 100, 150); // 50 (bounces back)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-let wrapY = gsap.utils.wrapYoyo(0, 100);[m
[31m-wrapY(150); // 50[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Best practices[m
[31m-[m
[31m-- ✅ Omit the value argument to get a reusable function when the same range/config is used many times (e.g. scroll handler, tween callback): `let mapFn = gsap.utils.mapRange(0, 1, 0, 360); mapFn(progress)`.[m
[31m-- ✅ Use **snap** for grid-aligned or step-based values; use **toArray** when GSAP or your code needs a real array from a selector or NodeList.[m
[31m-- ✅ Use **gsap.utils.selector(scope)** in components so selectors are scoped to a container or ref.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Do Not[m
[31m-[m
[31m-- ❌ Assume **mapRange** / **normalize** handle units; they work on numbers. Use **getUnit** / **unitize** when units matter.[m
[31m-- ❌ Override or rely on undocumented behavior; stick to the documented API.[m
[31m-[m
